Atlas overlay patch 0120: fed/fed-scheme
Why: fed://label.tld is the Federate Network's address (ADR 0052); the lead wants a real fed:// site open in
 Atlas. fed is only the transport (the helper atlas-fed verifies and hands back plain bytes), so the page is
 ordinary HTML in the ordinary renderer. Registering the scheme: standard (host-based origin, one site per
 Federate name), secure (a secure context, no mixed-content warning), CORS enabled and fetch()-able (a page
 loads its own files), web-safe (a link from one fed:// page to another is allowed), and a handled protocol
 (else the URL goes to the desktop's protocol handler). No cookie or storage scheme is registered: a fed page
 has none by default. Literals "fed" because chrome/common and chrome/renderer cannot include
 chrome/browser/atlas/fed.
Files: chrome/common/chrome_content_client.cc, chrome/renderer/chrome_content_renderer_client.cc, chrome/browser/profiles/profile_io_data.cc, chrome/browser/browser_process_impl.cc
Test filter: *AtlasFedUrl* (the scheme name); journey: fed://home.fed opens and its stylesheet loads
Rebase risk: L (a list entry and three one-line calls next to the chrome-search ones)
Release: 0.13
License: GPL-3.0-or-later (hunks in files the inherited series patches); BSD-3-Clause (hunks in the other engine files)
Verified at: 156.0.8078.79, base tree plus the patches before it in the series (dev slot 4)

--- a/chrome/common/chrome_content_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/common/chrome_content_client.cc
@@ -200,6 +200,8 @@
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID)
     content::kAndroidAppScheme,
 #endif
+    // Atlas: Federate Network addresses, fed://label.tld/path (ADR 0052).
+    "fed",
 };
 
 void ChromeContentClient::AddAdditionalSchemes(Schemes* schemes) {
@@ -230,6 +232,13 @@
   // chrome-search: resources shouldn't trigger insecure content warnings.
   schemes->secure_schemes.push_back(chrome::kChromeSearchScheme);
 
+  // Atlas: fed:// pages are verified by the Federate helper (a signed chain
+  // from a pinned root key), so they are a secure context, may fetch() their
+  // own files and give no mixed-content warnings. fed is only transport: the
+  // page is ordinary HTML in the ordinary renderer.
+  schemes->secure_schemes.push_back("fed");
+  schemes->cors_enabled_schemes.push_back("fed");
+
 #if BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_EXTENSIONS_CORE)
   // Treat extensions as secure because communication with them is entirely in
   // the browser, so there is no danger of manipulation or eavesdropping on
--- a/chrome/renderer/chrome_content_renderer_client.cc
+++ b/chrome/renderer/chrome_content_renderer_client.cc
@@ -619,6 +619,10 @@
   WebSecurityPolicy::RegisterURLSchemeAsSupportingFetchAPI(
       WebString::FromAscii(chrome::kChromeExperimentalSiteTokenProviderScheme));
 
+  // Atlas: a fed:// page may fetch() its own files.
+  WebSecurityPolicy::RegisterURLSchemeAsSupportingFetchAPI(
+      WebString::FromAscii("fed"));
+
   // This doesn't work in single-process mode.
   if (!base::CommandLine::ForCurrentProcess()->HasSwitch(
           switches::kSingleProcess)) {
